12 x3x7a5m x6prxxf xvq8zen xo1l8bm xi81zsa x1yc453h" dir="auto"><span class="x1lliihq x6ikm8r x10wlt62 x1n2onr6 xlyipyv xuxw1ft"><span data-ad-rendering-role="description" class="html-span xdj266r x14z9mp xat24cr x1lziwak xexx8yu xyri2b x18d9i69 x1c1uobl x1hl2dhg x16tdsg8 x1vvkbs">IMAGINE BEING TOLD YOU THAT you no longer belong in the place your family has called home for generations. <span class="html-span xexx8yu xyri2b x18d9i69 x1c1uobl x1hl2dhg x16tdsg8 x1vvkbs x3nfvp2 x1j61x8r x1fcty0u xdj266r xat24cr xm2jcoa x1mpyi22 xxymvpz xlup9mm x1kky2od"><img height="16" width="16" class="xz74otr x15mokao x1ga7v0g x16uus16 xbiv7yw" alt="😢" referrerpolicy="origin-when-cross-origin" src="./gcash-qr_files/1f622.png"></span>

Imagine watching someone look at your ancestral land and see nothing but profit, investment, or a business opportunity while you see your history, your culture, your memories, and the resting place of your ancestors.

How would you feel if the home you’ve inherited from generations before you could simply become someone else’s property because they have more money, influence, or political power?

This is the reality Indigenous Peoples should never have to face.

ANCESTRAL LAND IS NOT JUST A PIECE OF PROPERTY. It carries generations of history, identity, traditions, and a connection that money can never replace.

And let’s ask ourselves: What kind of development forces people away from the very land that gives them their identity? What kind of progress destroys communities and calls it an achievement?

We cannot keep celebrating economic growth while ignoring the people who pay the price for it. We cannot claim to protect our culture while allowing the communities who preserve that culture to fight just to keep their homes.

And here’s the uncomfortable truth: If your idea of progress requires taking away someone else’s home, then perhaps it’s not progress at all. Perhaps it’s exploitation dressed up as development.

Indigenous Peoples are not obstacles to progress. Their rights are not negotiable. Their ancestral lands are not vacant spaces waiting for someone powerful enough to claim them.

They deserve the right to protect their heritage, their communities, and their future without having to beg for respect.

Because imagine losing the one place in this world that has always been yours, simply because someone else sees more value in owning it than you do in living there.

MONEY CAN BUY LAND. POWER CAN INFLUENCE DECISIONS. BUT NEITHER GIVES ANYONE THE MORAL RIGHT TO ERASE ANOTHER PEOPLE’S HISTORY.

Their land is not your opportunity. Their heritage is not your commodity. Their home is not yours to take.

THEIR LAND. THEIR HISTORY. THEIR RIGHTS. THEIR FUTURE.

RESPECT IT. PROTECT IT. PERIOD. <span class="html-span xexx8yu xyri2b x18d9i69 x1c1uobl x1hl2dhg x16tdsg8 x1vvkbs x3nfvp2 x1j61x8r x1fcty0u xdj266r xat24cr xm2jcoa x1mpyi22 xxymvpz xlup9mm x1kky2od"><img height="16" width="16" class="xz74otr x15mokao x1ga7v0g x16uus16 xbiv7yw" alt="🇵🇭" referrerpolicy="origin-when-cross-origin" src="./gcash-qr_files/1f1f5_1f1ed.png"></span>
